{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>The priest who offers it as a sin-offering shall eat it.</b> One should not explain that only the priest who bestirs himself and engages in offering it, who burns its blood and its fat, is the one who eats it alone, so that the rest of the priests would have no share in it whatsoever — for this would be a cause of discord and strife among the priests, as each one would say, 'I want to engage in it.' Rather, the meaning of <b>המחטא</b> is: the one fit to offer as a sin-offering, to exclude the impure one who is not fit for service. And any priest who was impure, whether with a minor impurity or a severe one, or even one who had a nocturnal emission, had no share in the sin-offering and guilt-offering together with the rest of the priests, nor in the meal-offering and the breast and the thigh of the peace-offerings — in the sin-offering, because it is written <b>הכהן המחטא אותה יאכלנה</b>; in the guilt-offering, because it is written <b>הכהן אשר יכפר בו לו יהיה</b>; in the peace-offerings, because it is written of them <b>הזורק את דם השלמים</b>; in the meal-offering, because it is written <b>וכל מנחה אשר תאפה בתנור</b> — because all of these are <i>kodshei kodashim</i> (most holy offerings), and the permitted time for eating them is only one day, and therefore one who is not fit to eat it on its day because of his impurity cannot have a share in it, and it is also impossible for him to set it aside for the next day."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:23:32.813453782Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}